<h2>Common Claim Issues and Insurer Arguments</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cleaning vs. replacement.</strong> The most common dispute. Insurers often prefer cleaning; some materials, such as porous ceiling texture, insulation, and soft goods, may not be restorable.</li>
<li><strong>Limited scope.</strong> The insurer treats only the room where the fire occurred and not adjacent rooms or the attic.</li>
<li><strong>HVAC excluded from the scope.</strong> Duct cleaning or replacement is left out.</li>
<li><strong>Odor persistence.</strong> After cleaning, odor returns in heat and humidity, yet the file is closed.</li>
<li><strong>Sealing and painting.</strong> Only stained areas are painted rather than continuous wall and ceiling surfaces, leading to mismatch.</li>
<li><strong>Cause of smoke.</strong> Smoke from excluded sources, such as fireplaces or agricultural burning, is denied.</li>
</ul>

