<h2>Insurance Settlement Considerations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Deductible.</strong> Smoke from a fire usually falls under your all-other-perils deductible.</li>
<li><strong>ACV vs. RCV.</strong> Replaced contents and building materials may be paid at actual cash value first, with depreciation released after replacement. See <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">RCV vs. ACV in Texas</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Limits.</strong> Dwelling, contents, ALE, and special limits for valuables each apply separately.</li>
<li><strong>Ordinance or law.</strong> If smoke remediation requires replacing electrical or HVAC components, work must meet McAllen&rsquo;s 2024 ICC codes and the 2023 National Electrical Code effective January 1, 2026. Upgrade costs depend on ordinance or law coverage. See <a href="/local-building-codes/">McAllen building codes</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why Smoke Damage Is Easy to Underestimate</h2>
<p>Smoke follows air pressure and temperature differences, so it moves through attic spaces, wall cavities, electrical outlets, and the return side of the HVAC system. Rooms far from the fire can end up with fine soot inside closets and cabinets, on the tops of door frames, and in the ductwork. Because McAllen homes often run air conditioning nearly year-round, a system running during or after the fire can carry residue throughout the house.</p>
<p>Odor can also come back. Soot and smoke odor absorbed into porous materials such as insulation, drywall paper, carpet pad, and upholstery may seem gone after an initial cleaning, then return on hot, humid days. That is one reason it helps to document the property before and after cleaning.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Material</th><th>Cleaning often attempted</th><th>When replacement may be argued</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Painted walls and ceilings</td><td>Cleaning, sealing, repainting</td><td>Persistent staining or odor through sealer</td></tr>
<tr><td>Insulation</td><td>Rarely cleanable</td><td>Smoke-exposed attic insulation retains odor</td></tr>
<tr><td>Ductwork</td><td>Professional duct cleaning</td><td>Flexible or lined duct that cannot be cleaned effectively</td></tr>
<tr><td>Upholstery, mattresses, clothing</td><td>Ozone or specialty cleaning</td><td>Odor or staining remains after cleaning</td></tr>
<tr><td>Electronics</td><td>Specialty cleaning</td><td>Corrosion or failure from acidic residue</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>How a Public Adjuster May Help</h2>
<p>A public adjuster can document smoke migration throughout the property, review the cleaning protocol and results, compare the insurer&rsquo;s estimate against what restoration actually requires, organize the contents inventory, and present the claim. An <a href="/services/insurance-estimate-review/">insurance estimate review</a> can identify missing rooms or items; if the claim has already been paid, an <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">insurance claim supplement</a> may be appropriate. If the dispute is over the amount of loss, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">appraisal</a> may be an option. We cannot guarantee a result. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement.</p>

<h2>Policyholder Responsibilities</h2>
<ul>
<li>Report the smoke damage promptly, even if the fire was small or next door.</li>
<li>Take reasonable steps to limit further damage, such as replacing HVAC filters and ventilating the home if it is safe.</li>
<li>Keep damaged contents until documented, unless they pose a health risk.</li>
<li>Submit a proof of loss when requested, within the policy deadline.</li>
<li>Cooperate with inspections, testing, and document requests.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Applicable Policy Limitations and Exclusions</h2>
<p>Smoke claims may be limited by exclusions for smoke from agricultural smudging or industrial operations, gradual or repeated exposure (such as from a fireplace or candles), wear and tear, and pollutant or contaminant provisions. ALE has time or dollar limits, and depreciation recovery may depend on completing repairs within a stated period. Policies may also impose a deadline to file suit. For legal questions, consult a Texas attorney.</p>
